\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
General Equilibrium: Homework 2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider a pure-exchange economy with two consumers, 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

, and with two commodities, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u_{A} & = & \min[x_{A1},x_{A2}],\ \omega_{A}=(3,0)\\
u_{B} & = & \min[x_{B1},x_{B2}],\ \omega_{B}=(0,3)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Both consumption sets 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{A}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{B}$
\end_inset

 consists of integer points in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Show that 
\begin_inset Formula $p=(p^{1},p^{2})$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{1}=p^{2}$
\end_inset

 is a competitive equilibrium price.
 Calculate all competitive equilibrium allocations associated with this
 price vector.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Show whether (each of) the competitive equilibria is a (regular) Pareto
 optimum.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
State the First Theorem of Welfare Economics.
 Discuss how the results of part b conform with or vilate the First Theorem.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.

\series default
\bar default
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To find al the competitive equilibrium allocations, we must maximize the
 utility function of each consumer with respect to his budget constraint,
 and then verify the market clearing condition.
 Since each agent's preferences are monotone, then they are locally non-satiatie
d, and thus, Walras' Law holds.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p=p_{1}=p_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Now, both consumer's have identical maximization problems, so let us formulate
 the problem using a representative agent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Utility maximization for the representative agent:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max & u_{i}=\min[x_{i1},x_{i2}]\\
\mbox{s.t.} & px_{A1}+px_{A2}=3p
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Using the budget constraint, we obtain an expression for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i1}$
\end_inset

, which we then plug into the utility function:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
x_{i1} & = & 3-x_{i2}\\
3-x_{i2} & = & x_{i2}\\
x_{i2} & = & \frac{3}{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since the consumption set for both agents consists of integer points, then
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i2}=2$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i2}=1$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, for the price vector with 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}=p_{2}$
\end_inset

, we have several competitive equilibria:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left\{ \left[x_{1}^{*},x_{2}^{*},p^{*}\right]\right\}  & = & \left\{ \left[\left(\begin{array}{c}
2\\
1
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
2
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
1
\end{array}\right)\right],\left[\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
2
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
2\\
1
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
1
\end{array}\right)\right],\right.\\
 &  & \left[\left(\begin{array}{c}
2\\
1
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
1
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
1
\end{array}\right)\right],\left[\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
2
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
1
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
1
\end{array}\right)\right],\\
 &  & \left[\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
1
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
2\\
1
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
1
\end{array}\right)\right],\left[\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
1
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
2
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
1
\end{array}\right)\right],\\
 &  & \left.\left[\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
1
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
1
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
1
\end{array}\right)\right]\right\} 
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider the freasible allocation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x^{\prime}=\left[\left(\begin{array}{c}
2\\
2
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
1
\end{array}\right)\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 represents a Pareto improvement over all the competitive equilibrium allocation
s since agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is made better off without making agent 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 worse off.
 Therefore, none of the competitive equilibria are Pareto optimal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\emph on
(The First Fundamental Theorem of Welfare Economics) 
\emph default
If 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,p)$
\end_inset

 is a comeptitive equilibrium, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is weakly Pareto efficient, and further under local non-satiation, it is
 Pareto efficient.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note, one needs to pay attention to all of the assumptions, both implicit
 and/or explicit.
 The conditions for the theorem are: (i) local non-satiation; (ii) divisibilty;
 (iii) no externalitites; (iv) perfect competition; (v) complete information;
 etc.
 In this case, the problem outline clearly restricts the divisibilty of
 commoditites in the consumption set.
 Thus, a competitive equilibrium may not be Pareto efficient.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
In a pure exchange economy, consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 
\emph on
envies
\emph default
 consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}\succ_{i}x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 An allocation is called 
\emph on
envy free
\emph default
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\succsim_{i}x_{j}$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

.
 An allocation is called 
\emph on
fair
\emph default
 if it is envy free and Pareto efficient.
 In the following, assume that all consumption sets are equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{n}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 is the number of commodities.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Prove that envy free allocations always exist.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Prove, in an Edgeworth box for 
\begin_inset Formula $n=2$
\end_inset

, that envy free allocations need not be fair.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Assume that preferences are represented by utility functions that are continuous
, stricly increasing, and stricly quasi-concave.
 Prove that every exchange economy with a strictly positive aggregate endowment
 vector has at least one fair allocation.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.

\series default
\bar default
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let all agents have equal endowments, i.e., for any two agents 
\begin_inset Formula $\{i,j\}\subseteq N$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{i}=\omega_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 We will prove that if 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{*},p^{*})$
\end_inset

 constitutes a competitive equilibrium for a pure exchange economy, then
 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is envy-free.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Assume that all agents have equal endowments, i.e., for any two agents 
\begin_inset Formula $\{i,j\}\subseteq N$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{i}=\omega_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Also, assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{*},p^{*})$
\end_inset

 constitutes a competitive equilibrium for a pure exchange economy.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is envy free, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{i,j\}\subseteq N:x_{i}^{*}\succsim x_{j}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose for contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is not envy free.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ \{i,j\}\subseteq N:x_{j}^{*}\succ x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Note, the budget set for agent 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{j}(p)=\{x_{j}\in X_{j}:px_{j}\leq p\omega_{j}\}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}^{*}\in B_{j}(p)$
\end_inset

.
 Now, since 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{j}=\omega_{i}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{j}(p)=B_{i}(p)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}^{*}\in B_{i}(p)$
\end_inset

.
 However, this contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 maximizes agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's utility, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{*}\in x_{i}^{\succsim}(p)$
\end_inset

 .
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is envy free.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Assume that preferences are represented by utility functions that are continuous
, stricly increasing, and stricly quasi-concave.
 Then, preferences are continuous, stricly monotone, and stricly convex.
 Take a pure exchange economy 
\begin_inset Formula $e=\left(\{X_{i},\succsim_{i},\omega_{i}\}_{i\in N}\right)$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\omega}={\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\omega_{i}>0$
\end_inset

.
 We want to show that this economy has at least one fair allocation.
 Since we are dealing with optimality concepts, initial endowments can be
 redistributed among agents in a society.
 So, we redistribute individual endowments so that all agents have an equal
 share of each commodity.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{i}=\overline{\omega}=\frac{\hat{\omega}}{n}\ \forall\ i\in N$
\end_inset

.
 By Theorem 7.4.6, there exists a competitive equilibrium 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{*},p^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 Now, at this point, all individuals' income is the same, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\omega_{1}=\cdots=p^{*}\omega_{n}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, by Theorem 9.3.1, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is a stricly fair allocation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider a pure exchange economy with 
\begin_inset Formula $n=2$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{A}=X_{B}=\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{A}=(1,0)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{B}=(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose the consumers have the following utility functions:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u_{A}(x_{A1},x_{A2}) & = & x_{A1}+2x_{A2}\\
u_{B}(x_{B1},x_{B2}) & = & 2x_{B1}+x_{B2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Draw offer curves in the Edgeworth Box.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Find the set of Pareto optimal allocation and draw them in the Edgeworth
 Box.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Calculate the competitive equilibrium with the price of good 1 is equal
 to 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Calculate the set of positive welfare weights 
\begin_inset Formula $(\alpha_{A},\alpha_{B})$
\end_inset

, such that the equilibrium allocation maximizes the welfare function 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\alpha_{A}u_{A}(x_{A1},x_{A2})+\alpha_{B}u_{B}(x_{B1},x_{B2})
\]

\end_inset

among all feasible allocations 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{A1},x_{A2},x_{B1},x_{B2})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 be the welfare function defined by the equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
W(\hat{\omega}_{1},\hat{\omega}_{2})={\displaystyle \max_{\{x_{A},x_{B}\}}u_{A}(x_{A1},x_{A2})+u_{B}(x_{B1},x_{B2})}
\]

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}+x_{B}\leq(\hat{\omega}_{1},\hat{\omega}_{2})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A},x_{B}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 What are 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial W(1,1)}{\partial\hat{\omega}_{1}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial W(1,1)}{\partial\hat{\omega}_{2}}$
\end_inset

?
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.

\series default
\bar default
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To find offer curves, we must maximize the utility function of each consumer
 with respect to his budget constraint.
 Since each agent's preferences are monotone, then they are locally non-satiatie
d, and thus, Walras' Law holds.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p=\frac{p_{2}}{p_{1}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The constrained utility maximization problem for agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max & u_{A}(x_{A1},x_{A2})=x_{A1}+2x_{A2}\\
\mbox{s.t} & x_{A1}+px_{A2}=1
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, the demand function for agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{A}(p)=\begin{cases}
\left(\begin{array}{c}
0\\
\frac{1}{p}
\end{array}\right) & \mbox{if}\ p<2\\
\left(\begin{array}{c}
x_{A1}\\
\frac{1}{2}-\frac{x_{A1}}{2}
\end{array}\right),x_{A1}\in\left[0,1\right] & \mbox{if}\ p=2\\
\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
0
\end{array}\right) & \mbox{if}\ p>2
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

The constrained utility maximization problem for agent 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max & u_{B}(x_{B1},x_{B2})=2x_{B1}+x_{B2}\\
\mbox{s.t.} & x_{B1}+px_{B2}=p
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, the demand function for agent 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{B}(p)=\begin{cases}
\left(\begin{array}{c}
0\\
1
\end{array}\right) & \mbox{if}\ p<\frac{1}{2}\\
\left(\begin{array}{c}
x_{B1}\\
1-2x_{B1}
\end{array}\right),x_{B1}\in\left[0,\frac{1}{2}\right] & \mbox{if}\ p=\frac{1}{2}\\
\left(\begin{array}{c}
p\\
0
\end{array}\right) & \mbox{if}\ p>\frac{1}{2}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(x_{i})$
\end_inset

 is differentiable and quasi-concave, then a necessary and sufficient condition
 for the interior points to be Parteo optimal (PO) is that 
\begin_inset Formula $MRS_{x_{A1,}x_{A2}}=MRS_{x_{B1},x_{B2}}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $MRS_{x_{A1,}x_{A2}}=\frac{1}{2}\neq2=MRS_{x_{B1},x_{B2}}$
\end_inset

, then there are no interior PO points.
 However, because indifference curves are linear and agent 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

's indifference curves are steeper, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $MRS_{A}<MRS_{B}$
\end_inset

, then the set of Pareto efficient allocations is given by the upper and
 left edges of the Edgeworth box.
 Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
PO=\{(x_{A},x_{B})\in X_{A}\times X_{B}:x_{A1}x_{B2}=0\ \wedge\ x_{A}+x_{B}=(1,1)\}
\]

\end_inset

is the set of Pareto optimal allocations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In part a, we found the demand function for each agent after normalizing
 the prices by setting the price of good 1 equal to 1.
 To find the competitive equilibrium, we must find all 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)=\hat{x}(p)-\hat{w}=0$
\end_inset

.
 By Walras' Law, it is enough to show only one markets clearing for 
\begin_inset Formula $L=2$
\end_inset

.
 Let us consider the two cases for 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
 Now, we will consider the various cases given by the functions to determine
 the competitive equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
\bar under
Case:
\emph default
\bar default
 
\begin_inset Formula $p<\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}(p)=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
0 & \frac{1}{p}\end{array}\right)^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{B}(p)=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
0 & 1\end{array}\right)^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{2}(p)>\hat{\omega}_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, a competitive equilibrium fails to exist for this case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
\bar under
Case:
\emph default
\bar default
 
\begin_inset Formula $p=\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}(p)=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
0 & 2\end{array}\right)^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{B}(p)=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
x_{B1} & 1-2x_{B1}\end{array}\right)^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{B1}\in\left[0,\frac{1}{2}\right]$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{2}(p)>\hat{\omega}_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, a competitive equilibrium fails to exist for this case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
\bar under
Case:
\emph default
\bar default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2}<p<2$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}(p)=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
0 & \frac{1}{p}\end{array}\right)^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{B}(p)=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
p & 0\end{array}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $p=1$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}=\hat{\omega}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, a competitive equilibrium is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[x_{A}^{*},x_{B}^{*},p^{*}\right]=\left[\left(\begin{array}{c}
0\\
1
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
0
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
1
\end{array}\right)\right]
\]

\end_inset


\emph on
\bar under
Case:
\emph default
\bar default
 
\begin_inset Formula $p=2$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}(p)=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
x_{A1} & \frac{1}{2}-\frac{x_{A1}}{2}\end{array}\right)^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A1}\in\left[0,2\right]$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{B}(p)=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
2 & 0\end{array}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{1}(p)>\hat{\omega}_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, a competitive equilibrium fails to exist for this case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
\bar under
Case:
\emph default
\bar default
 
\begin_inset Formula $p>2$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}(p)=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 0\end{array}\right)^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{B}(p)=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
p & 0\end{array}\right)^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{1}(p)>\hat{\omega}_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, a competitive equilibrium fails to exist for this case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part d.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since preferences are locally non-satiated, then by the First Fundamental
 Theorem of Welfare Economics, the competitive equilibrium allocation given
 in part c, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

, is Pareto optimal.
 Since both agent's utility functions are linear, then they are also concave,
 differentiable, and stricly monotone.
 So, by Proposition 8.7.2, there exists some choice of weights 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 maximizes the social welfare function:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
W(x_{A},x_{B})=\alpha_{A}(x_{A1}+2x_{A2})+\alpha_{B}(2x_{B1}+x_{B2})
\]

\end_inset

Feasibility and local non-satiation imply
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{flalign*}
x_{A1}+x_{B1} & =1\implies x_{B1}=1-x_{A1}\\
x_{A2}+x_{B2} & =1\implies x_{B2}=1-x_{A2}
\end{flalign*}

\end_inset

Also, since 
\begin_inset Formula $0\leq x_{Ai}\leq1,\ i=1,2$
\end_inset

, then we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
W(x_{A},x_{B}) & = & \alpha_{A}(x_{A1}+2x_{A2})+\alpha_{B}(2-2x_{A1}+1-x_{A2})\\
W(x_{A},x_{B}) & = & \alpha_{A}x_{A1}+2\alpha_{A}x_{A2}+2\alpha_{B}-2\alpha_{B}x_{A1}+\alpha_{B}-\alpha_{B}x_{A2}\\
W(x_{A},x_{B}) & = & x_{A1}(\alpha_{A}-2\alpha_{B})+x_{A2}(2\alpha_{A}-\alpha_{B})+3\alpha_{B}\geq0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So, the competitive equilibrium allocation will be chosen when 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{A}-2\alpha_{B}\leq0\iff\alpha_{A}\leq2\alpha_{B}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $2\alpha_{A}-\alpha_{B}\geq0\iff\alpha_{A}\geq\frac{\alpha_{B}}{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, we must have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\alpha_{B}}{2}\leq\alpha_{A}\leq2\alpha_{B}
\]

\end_inset

 
\series bold
\bar under
Part e.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 be the welfare function defined by the equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
W(\hat{\omega}_{1},\hat{\omega}_{2})={\displaystyle \max_{\{x_{A},x_{B}\}}u_{A}(x_{A1},x_{A2})+u_{B}(x_{B1},x_{B2})}
\]

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}+x_{B}\leq(\hat{\omega}_{1},\hat{\omega}_{2})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A},x_{B}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Note, the right hand side of this equation is maximized at the competitive
 equilibrium allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

, i.e., when 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A1}=0=x_{B2}$
\end_inset

.
 Also, since preferences are locally non-satiated, the feasibility constraints
 hold with equality.
 Thus, the welfare function can be rewritten as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
W(\hat{\omega}_{1},\hat{\omega}_{2})=2x_{A2}+2x_{B1}
\]

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A2}=\hat{\omega}_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{B1}=\hat{\omega}_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 We substitute these constraints into the welfare function to obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
W(\hat{\omega}_{1},\hat{\omega}_{2})=2\hat{\omega}_{2}+2\hat{\omega}_{1}
\]

\end_inset

Therefore, the partial derivatives of 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

evaluated at 
\begin_inset Formula $(1,1)$
\end_inset

 are:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{\partial W(1,1)}{\partial\hat{\omega}_{1}} & = & 2\\
\frac{\partial W(1,1)}{\partial\hat{\omega}_{2}} & = & 2
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
(Cornwall) In an economy with two types of consumers, each type has the
 respective utility function and endowments:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{1}(x_{1},y_{1})=x_{1}y_{1}\ \mbox{and}\ \omega_{1}=(8,2)
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{2}(x_{2},y_{2})=x_{2}y_{2}\ \mbox{and}\ \omega_{2}=(2,8)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Draw an Edgeworth Box for this economy when there is one consumer of each
 type.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Characterize as precisely as possible the set of allocations that are in
 the core of this two-consumer economy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Show that the allocation giving 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{1},y_{1})=(4,4)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{2},y_{2})=(6,6)$
\end_inset

 is in the core.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Now replicate this economy once so there are two consumers of each type,
 for a total of four consumers in the economy.
 Show that the double copy of the previous allocation is 
\emph on
not
\emph default
 in the core of the replicated economy.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To simplfy notation, let 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 represent the consumption vector for agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}\equiv(x_{i},y_{i})^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, so a specific allocation is represented by 
\begin_inset Formula $z\equiv\left[(x_{1},y_{1}),(x_{2},y_{2})\right]$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $n=2$
\end_inset

 and the preference relations are monotonic, then an allocation is in the
 core if and only if it is Pareto optimal (PO) and individually rational
 (IR).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, let us characterize the set of PO allocations.
 Important to note, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 are differentiable and quasi-concave.
 So, an allocation is PO if it is feasible, and a necessary and sufficient
 condition for interior points is that 
\begin_inset Formula $MRS_{x_{1},y_{1}}=MRS_{x_{2},y_{2}}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the set of interior PO allocations is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
PO=\left\{ (z_{1},z_{2})\in X_{1}\times X_{2}:z_{1}+z_{2}\leq(10,10)^{\prime}\ \wedge\ \frac{y_{1}}{x_{1}}=\frac{y_{2}}{x_{2}}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

Next, let us characterize the set of IR allocations.
 An allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 is individually rational if 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N:z_{i}\succsim_{i}\omega_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

, then an allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 is IR if 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}y_{1}\geq16$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}y_{2}\geq16$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, the set of core allocations is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
PO\cap IR=\left\{ (z_{1},z_{2})\in X_{1}\times X_{2}:z_{1}+z_{2}\leq(10,10)^{\prime},\frac{y_{1}}{x_{1}}=\frac{y_{2}}{x_{2}},x_{1}y_{1}\geq16,x_{2}y_{2}\geq16\right\} 
\]

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here, we have an allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 giving 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{1},y_{1})=(4,4)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{2},y_{2})=(6,6)$
\end_inset

.
 Let us verify that 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 is in the core.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left(\begin{array}{c}
4\\
4
\end{array}\right)+\left(\begin{array}{c}
6\\
6
\end{array}\right) & = & \left(\begin{array}{c}
10\\
10
\end{array}\right)\\
\frac{4}{4} & = & \frac{6}{6}\\
4\cdot4 & = & 16\\
6\cdot6 & = & 32
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, the allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 is in the core.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part d.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, replicate this economy so there are two consumers of each type, for
 a total of four consumers in the economy.
 We now have consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 of the first agent type, and consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 of the second agent type.
 The endowments of each agent are
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\omega_{1A}=\left(\begin{array}{c}
8\\
2
\end{array}\right),\omega_{1B}=\left(\begin{array}{c}
8\\
2
\end{array}\right),\omega_{2A}=\left(\begin{array}{c}
2\\
8
\end{array}\right),\omega_{2B}=\left(\begin{array}{c}
2\\
8
\end{array}\right)
\]

\end_inset

while the double copy of the previous allocation, 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

, is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
z_{1A}=\left(\begin{array}{c}
4\\
4
\end{array}\right),z_{1B}=\left(\begin{array}{c}
4\\
4
\end{array}\right),z_{2A}\left(\begin{array}{c}
6\\
6
\end{array}\right),z_{2B}=\left(\begin{array}{c}
6\\
6
\end{array}\right)
\]

\end_inset

Note, a feasible allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 is said to have the core property if it cannot be improved upon for any
 coalition, i.e., if 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 has no blocking coalitions.
 So, let agents 1A, 1B, and 2A form a coalition 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 with their own endowments.
 Now, within this coalition, consider the allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $z^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
z_{1A}^{\prime}=\left(\begin{array}{c}
6\\
3
\end{array}\right),z_{1B}^{\prime}=\left(\begin{array}{c}
6\\
3
\end{array}\right),z_{2A}^{\prime}=\left(\begin{array}{c}
6\\
6
\end{array}\right)
\]

\end_inset

The allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $z^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 represents a Pareto improvement for the allocation since
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{i\in S}z_{i}^{\prime}=\left(\begin{array}{c}
18\\
12
\end{array}\right)=\sum_{i\in S}\omega_{i}
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u_{1A}(6,3) & > & u_{1A}(4,4)\\
u_{1B}(6,3) & > & u_{1B}(4,4)\\
u_{2A}(6,6) & = & u_{2A}(6,6)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, there exists a blocking coalition to the original allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 is not in the core of the replicated economy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider a society consisting of 25 individuals.
 The society has four political parties called A, B, C, and D.
 The preferences of the individuals in the society are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
9 people rank parties in the following order: A first, B second, C third,
 and D fourth.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
5 people rank parties in the following order: B first, A second, C third,
 and D fourth.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
1 person ranks parties in the following order: C first, B second, D third,
 and A fourth.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
10 people rank parties in the following order: D first, C second, B third,
 and A fourth.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Are these preferences single peaked?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Is there a Condorcet winner? Find the Condorcet winner or prove that it
 does not exist.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, let 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 be a set containing the four political parties, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\{A,B,C,D\}$
\end_inset

.
 Next, we define a binary relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $A\geq B\geq C\geq D$
\end_inset

.
 This binary relation is a linear order since it is reflexive, transitive,
 and total.
 Now, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 is said to be 
\emph on
single-peaked
\emph default
 with respect to the linear order 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 if there is an alternative 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in X$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 is increasing with respect 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq$
\end_inset

 on the lower contour set 
\begin_inset Formula $L(x)=\{y\in X:y\leq x\}$
\end_inset

 and decreasing with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq$
\end_inset

 on the upper contour set 
\begin_inset Formula $U(x)=\{y\in X:y\geq x\}$
\end_inset

.
 We want to see if the preferences of the individuals in the society are
 single-peaked.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
\bar under
Group 1:
\emph default
\bar default
 Consider 
\begin_inset Formula $A\in X$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $A\succ_{1}B\succ_{1}C\succ_{1}D$
\end_inset

, then preferences are increasing with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq$
\end_inset

 on the lower contour set 
\begin_inset Formula $L(A)=\{A,B,C,D\}$
\end_inset

.
 Preferences are neither increasing nor decreasing on the upper contour
 set 
\begin_inset Formula $U(A)=A$
\end_inset

 since 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is the peak of satisfaction.
 Thus, group 1's preferences are single-peaked.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
\bar under
Group 2:
\emph default
\bar default
 Consider 
\begin_inset Formula $B\in X$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $B\succ_{2}C\succ_{2}D$
\end_inset

, then preferences are increasing with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq$
\end_inset

 on the lower contour set 
\begin_inset Formula $L(B)=\{B,C,D\}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $B\succ_{2}A$
\end_inset

, then preferences are decreasing with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq$
\end_inset

 on the upper contour set 
\begin_inset Formula $U(B)=\{A,B\}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, group 2's preferences are single-peaked, and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is group 2's peak.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
\bar under
Group 3:
\emph default
\bar default
 Consider 
\begin_inset Formula $C\in X$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $C\succ_{3}D$
\end_inset

, then preferences are increasing with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq$
\end_inset

 on the lower contour set 
\begin_inset Formula $L(C)=\{C,D\}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $C\succ_{3}B\succ_{3}A$
\end_inset

, then preferences are decreasing with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq$
\end_inset

 on the upper contour set 
\begin_inset Formula $U(C)=\{A,B,C\}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, group 3's preferences are single-peaked, and 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 is group 3's peak.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
\bar under
Group 4:
\emph default
\bar default
 Consider 
\begin_inset Formula $D\in X$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $D\succ_{4}C\succ_{4}B\succ_{4}A$
\end_inset

, then preferences are increasing with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq$
\end_inset

 on the lower contour set 
\begin_inset Formula $L(D)=\{D\}$
\end_inset

, and they are decreasing with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq$
\end_inset

 on the upper contour set 
\begin_inset Formula $U(D)=\{A,B,C,D\}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset

group 4's preferences are single-peaked, and 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 is group 4's peak.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, the preferences of all four groups are single-peaked.
 Therefore, all individuals in the society have single-peaked preferences
 with respect to the linear order 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given the preference profile above, let 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 be the maximal alternative for 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq$
\end_inset

 is a linear order on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 is single-peaked 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N$
\end_inset

, then by Proposition 9.4.1, the majority rule 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{F}(\succsim)$
\end_inset

 is aggregatable:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{h}\ \tilde{F}(\succsim)\ y,\ \ \forall\ y\in X
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{h}$
\end_inset

 is the peak of the median agent.
 So, the peak of the median agent is socially optimal by majority voting,
 and any alternative having this property is called a Condorcet winner.
 Now, agent 
\begin_inset Formula $h\in N$
\end_inset

 is a median agent for the given preference profile if 
\begin_inset Formula $|\{i\in N:x_{i}\geq x_{h}\}|\geq\frac{N}{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $|\{i\in N:x_{h}\geq x_{i}\}|\geq\frac{N}{2}$
\end_inset

.
 An agent from group 2 will be the median voter since 
\begin_inset Formula $|\{i\in N:x_{i}\geq B\}|=14\geq\frac{25}{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $|\{i\in N:B\geq x_{i}\}|=16\geq\frac{25}{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, alternative 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is the Condorcet winner.
\end_layout

